(
        '20499',
        'AML system issues',
        'Dear colleagues,    Reference to our teams meeting today please find below notes to be discussed with the vendor Biotech and provide us with a clear solution / answers    •Workflow arrangement / since it was labeled with a system username not administrator whom created the alert as usual?  •Workflow arrangement / after we take some samples from AML closed report to compare it on the system, we didn’t find any mentioned user who take final step on those cases  •Difference between date alert started and ended since we found unusual durations regarding (AML closed alerts report) generated by the system.  •In a sample case unfortunately, the system showed that alert processed by anther user (noting that we chose this sample from AML closed alerts) so it supposed to be this case finalized in level two  Noting that we take this case Id and rechecked from that user which is mine (dana.khasro) we found nothing / attached screen.    All screen shots related to above points sent already.  Your prompt response much appreciated since EY team waiting our action regarding this matter.    Thankyou in advance.',
        'Based on our web session, please review the points below carefully. The questions raised indicate a fundamental misunderstanding of how the system operates, so the following clarifications should settle the matter from an AML operational and system-logic perspective:  1. End Day Column Request:  Before any action can be taken, you must provide the complete business requirement for the “End Day” column, including purpose, logic, and expected usage. Without full details, this request cannot be forwarded to the development team.  2. Suspected Customer Screen:  The Suspected Customer Screen is not an alert or case-management screen. It displays the number of customers at the bank from the Manager level. It should not be compared to AML alert reports, as the two serve entirely different functions.  3. Locked By Field:  The “Locked By” field simply identifies the first user who opened the alert. Once opened, the alert becomes locked to that user. This is standard AML policy to ensure proper case ownership and prevent unauthorized handling.  4. Duplicate Alert Concern:  From the 24th until today, there have been no duplicate alerts. The situation you referred to involves one customer matched under two different nicknames linked to the same SDN entry ID. This is correct system behavior and not a duplication issue.  5. System Access and Workflow:  The system is functioning exactly as designed. When an alert is locked or closed, only the assigned user can access it. This restriction is deliberate and required for compliance and proper audit trails.  6. Clarification of Misinterpretation:  The discrepancies you highlighted stem from comparing unrelated data sources. Once the correct purpose of each screen/report is understood, it becomes clear that the system is producing accurate and consistent results. There is no system issue in this regard.  Please don’t hesitate to contact us if you have any concerns',
